@page "/"
@rendermode InteractiveServer

<PageTitle>App 1: Data Binding Demo</PageTitle>

<div class="glass-card animate-fade-in">
    <h2>🔗 Application 01: Data Binding Demonstration</h2>
    <p class="text-secondary">Explore the differences between one-way and two-way data binding in Blazor.</p>
    
    <div class="form-group mt-4">
        <label for="nameInput" class="form-label">Enter your name (Two-Way Bound):</label>
        <input id="nameInput" type="text" class="form-control" @bind="userName" placeholder="Type a name here..." />
    </div>

    <div class="alert-premium alert-info mt-4">
        <span>👋</span>
        <div>
            <strong>Greeting (One-Way Bound):</strong> 
            @if (string.IsNullOrWhiteSpace(userName))
            {
                <span>Hello, Guest! Please enter your name above.</span>
            }
            else
            {
                <span>Hello, @userName! Welcome to our premium Blazor dashboard.</span>
            }
        </div>
    </div>

    <div class="concept-card mt-4">
        <h4>📚 Binding Concepts Explained</h4>
        <hr class="my-2" style="border-color: var(--border-color);" />
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Two-Way Data Binding:</strong> Implemented on the text input using <code>@@bind="userName"</code>. 
                This establishes a bi-directional sync: typing in the text input immediately updates the C# <code>userName</code> property, 
                and any updates to <code>userName</code> in C# code are instantly pushed back to update the input's value in the browser.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>One-Way Data Binding:</strong> Implemented in the greeting message using <code>@@userName</code>. 
                This is a uni-directional flow from C# to HTML. When the backing state changes, Blazor automatically re-renders the component, 
                pulling the new value of <code>userName</code> and displaying it inside the HTML markup.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </div>
</div>

@code {
    private string userName { get; set; } = string.Empty;
}
